{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_5alteupaq55c3nutucv5vscakkwtcu6py3rvlvtmxihkz7fza5pa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Different. Hmm. Maybe in the C, the blake256_update doesn't get the value it ought to, but it's still finalized and produces a digest. Let me trace whether C's blake256_update actually has different behavior than mine.\n\nLooking at C blake256_update more carefully:\n```c\nvoid blake256_update( blakestate256 *S, const u8 *data, u64 datalen ) {\n\n  int left=S->buflen >> 3; \n  int fill=64 - left;\n   \n  if( left && ( ((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) >= fill ) ) {\n    memcpy( S->buf + left, data, fill );\n    S->t[0] += 512;\n    if (S->t[0] == 0) S->t[1]++;      \n    blake256_compress( S, S->buf );\n    data += fill;\n    datalen  -= (fill << 3);       \n    left = 0;\n  }\n\n  while( datalen >= 512 ) {\n    S->t[0] += 512;\n    if (S->t[0] == 0) S->t[1]++;\n    blake256_compress( S, data );\n    data += 64;\n    datalen -= 512;\n  }\n  \n  if( datalen > 0 ) {\n    memcpy( S->buf + left, data, datalen>>3 );\n    S->buflen = (left<<3) + datalen;\n  }\n  else S->buflen=0;\n}\n```\n\nWhen called with `(data, 23)` (bit count), datalen >> 3 = 2 bytes. So 2 bytes of data are written into S->buf, S->buflen = 23.\n\nWhen called next with `(sep, 1)` (bit count 1), datalen >> 3 = 0 bytes. But then `else S->buflen = 0` since datalen=1 > 0 hits the if datalen>0 branch — `nbytes = 1>>3 = 0`. memcpy of 0 bytes (no-op). S->buflen = 23 + 1 = 24.\n\nSo even with the buggy \"bit count\" interpretation, it works incrementally. Now for the call `blake256_update(&S, msg, 33)` with mlen=33 (bit count interpreted): nbytes = 33>>3 = 4. So 4 bytes of msg get added. State: buflen=24+33=57 bits = 7 bytes (4 from msg, 2 from \"tag\", 0 from \"sep\"). Wait, buflen is still bit count.\n\nThis is all weird. The key thing is that the C is consistent in its (buggy) usage, so we just need to match that behavior. Let me step through more carefully.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-read the C blake256_update more carefully. The first \"if left && fill\" branch uses `fill` bytes - so it also assumes datalen >> 3 bytes are present. So it's really a bytes-input function disguised as bits.\n\nLet me test this in C: if I pass 16 bytes of data with `datalen = 16`, it writes... well left starts at 0, left=0, so first if skipped. while datalen>=512 false. if datalen>0: nbytes = 16>>3 = 2. So writes 2 bytes! The other 14 bytes are ignored. But state.buflen = 0+16 = 16.\n\nSo next call with same: left = 16>>3 = 2 bytes filled in buf. fill = 62. (16>>3) & 0x3F = 2 (=== fill 62? No). False. while skipped. datalen>0: nbytes = 2. Writes data[..2] to buf[2..4]. buflen = 16+16=32.\n\nAt this rate buflen grows by 16 each time. Eventually buflen could reach over 64*8=512 which is 64 bytes worth. Actually buflen is in bits and indexes via `S->buflen >> 3 = bytes`. The buf array is 64 bytes.\n\nHmm, but if buflen gets to 64*8=512, then `left = 64`. Then `state.buf[left + nbytes]` panics since buf is only 64 bytes.\n\nActually, after two updates, buflen=32, then in third call to update with datalen=16: left=32>>3=4 bytes. nbytes=2. buf[4..6]. buflen=48. 4th: left=6, buf[6..8], buflen=64. 5th: left=8, buf[8..10], buflen=80. ...\n\nWait, the data is being written up to buf[left + nbytes]. nbytes=2 always. left grows by 2 each call. After 32 calls, left=64. Then writing to buf[64..66] panics!\n\nBut we only call blake update a few times... let me count for 128s gen_message_random:\n- update sk_prf 16 — buflen=16\n- update optrand 16 — buflen=32\n- update m mlen — for mlen=33 — datalen=33, left=4, fill=60, condition (33>>3)=4 < 60 false. While 33<512. datalen>0 nbytes=4. buf[4..8]. buflen=4*8+33=65.\n- final: msglen 8 bytes, etc.\n\nProbably manageable. But for `mlen` larger... let me check the panic. Oh wait the panic was at `index 14, len 14` in src/blake.rs:371. Let me find line 371:"}],"stop_reason":n