{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_z3wxlzvpiomkbejaugmvn6vqc74agtgw6j6eu4nkghcbvfoxv2ra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Different. Let me check the round structure. The C uses ROUND macros that have a specific order: g256 calls in column then diagonal. Looking at the C blake256 ROUND macro line 127-239, I see calls with:\n- `v0 += m0^c0; v0+=v4; v12^=v0; ...` (G0)\n- `v1 += m2^c2; v1+=v5; v13^=v1; ...` (G1)\n- `v2 += m4^c4; ...` (G2)\n- `v3 += m6^c6; ...` (G3)\n- then `v2 += m5^c5; ...` (continuing G2)\n- `v3 += m7^c7; ...` (continuing G3)\n- `v1 += m3^c3; ...` (continuing G1)\n- `v0 += m1^c1; ...` (continuing G0)\n- then diagonal G4: `v0 += m8^c8; v0+=v5; v15^=v0;` ...\n\nSo the order of (m,c) pairs: m0/c0, m2/c2, m4/c4, m6/c6, m5/c5, m7/c7, m3/c3, m1/c1, m8/c8, m10/c10, m12/c12, m14/c14, m13/c13, m15/c15, m11/c11, m9/c9.\n\nThe first ROUND is `ROUND(m0,cst[1],m1,cst[0],m2,cst[3],m3,cst[2],...)`. So the macro arguments are:\n- m0=m0, c0=cst[1]\n- m1=m1, c1=cst[0]\n- m2=m2, c2=cst[3]\n- m3=m3, c3=cst[2]\n- m4=m4, c4=cst[5]\n- m5=m5, c5=cst[4]\n- ...\n\nSo sigma row 0 maps to m indices [0,1,2,3,4,5,...,15] and sigma's pairing of (m,c) is via this:\n- Position 0: g256(v[0,4,8,12], m0^c0=m[s[0]]^cst[s[1]]); next iteration of first G half: m1^c1=m[s[1]]^cst[s[0]].\n\nSo g256 mixing of (a,b,c,d): v[a]+=m^c, v[d]^=v[a] rot, etc. The C ROUND macro does TWO calls of this G function for each column (one with m0,c0 and one with m1,c1).\n\nLooking at my Rust g256 — it does the FULL G mix (4 mixing operations) in one call. But the C ROUND has 8 partial mixings (each partial mix is 4 operations with one m^c addition). Wait no: my Rust g256:\n```rust\nv[a] += mi^ki + v[b]; v[d] = rot(v[d]^v[a],16); v[c]+=v[d]; v[b] = rot(v[b]^v[c],12);\nv[a] += v[b]; v[d] = rot(v[d]^v[a],8); v[c]+=v[d]; v[b] = rot(v[b]^v[c],7);\n```\n\nThat's a \"full G\" with ONE m-c addition. But C ROUND has:\n```c\nv0 += m0 ^ c0;  // first partial: just adds m^c\nv0 += v4;\nv12 ^= v0; v12 = ROT(v12,16);\nv8 += v12; v4 ^= v8; v4 = ROT(v4,12);\n// ... interleaved with v1,v2,v3...\nv0 += m1 ^ c1;  // SECOND partial: same column but new m^c\nv0 += v4;\nv12 ^= v0; v12 = ROT(v12,8);\nv8 += v12; v4 ^= v8; v4 = ROT(v4,7);\n```\n\nSo actually each G function in C does TWO m^c additions (one for each \"half\"). Let me look at the BLAKE-256 spec... right, the standard BLAKE compression's G function has TWO m additions per round. So G(a,b,c,d, m_{σ(2i)}, m_{σ(2i+1)}, c_{σ(2i)}, c_{σ(2i+1)}).\n\nMy Rust g256 only takes one m and one c. I'm missing the second.\n\nLet me fix this. Actually looking at `g256` then `g256` calls in my code:\n```rust\ng256(v, 0, 4, 8, 12, m[s[0]], CST256[s[1]]);\ng256(v, 0, 4, 8, 12, m[s[1]], CST256[s[0]]);\n```\n\nTwo consecutive g256 calls on same column with different m,c. But each g256 does the FULL G operation. So I'm doing G twice on same column when only ONE G is supposed to happen with both messages.\n\nThe BLAKE G function:\n```\nG_i(a, b, c, d):\n  a := a + b + (m_{σ_i(2r)} XOR c_{σ_i(2r+1)})\n  d := (d XOR a) >>> 16\n  c := c + d\n  b := (b XOR c) >>> 12\n  a := a + b + (m_{σ_i(2r+1)} XOR c_{σ_i(2r)})\n  d := (d XOR a) >>> 8\n  c := c + d\n  b := (b XOR c) >>> 7\n```\n\nSo G has TWO message additions in a single G call. Let me fix:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":673,"cache_read_input_tokens":322073,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":673,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"74d25225-68a6-45de-a328-809a2a520fd3","uuid":"6a93a16e-e2d1-4bd3-90b7-f2c100d46dcc"}
